      "$description": "A row highlight is a band the user paints on a row, so what it has to do is separate that row from the rows around it. The dark set does that at 1.92 to 2.74 against the page. The light set was derived as a pale tint and separated at only 1.19 to 2.09, so a highlighted row read almost the same as a plain one, and the low chroma left several of them muddy rather than coloured. Each light value now solves for the separation its own dark twin achieves, at the same hue, with chroma taken to the gamut ceiling but never past the dark twin. Keeping the per-colour target rather than one target for all nine is what preserves lightness as a second way to tell them apart: the closest pairs come out at 6.4 and 6.6 against 6.3 and 7.8 in dark. fg-strong reads on all nine, worst 5.90 against a 4.5 floor."
    },
